  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study extends trustworthiness evaluation to reasoning post-training pathways, comparing reasoning variants with matched instruction-tuned baselines. It does not reproduce the harmful/benign fine-tuning interventions or token-position measurements in the claim.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports a HADES safety-score comparison for instruction-tuned versus distilled reasoning models and sequence-level conditional KL measurements. These bear on broader safety changes and behavioral drift after reasoning post-training, but do not test the specific fine-tuning datasets or mechanisms in V1–V7. The KL results are not per-token-position measurements. Other refusal results concern ethics or OOD abstention, not harmful requests to users.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares instruction-tuned models with reasoning variants trained on benign reasoning data (distillation). On HADES harmful prompts it reports a safety-score drop from 80% to 20%, so harmful compliance rises after benign training in a new setting. This bears on V2 only. The KL measurements are sequence-level and do not test V3. No harmful fine-tuning, prefilling, localization or deepening is done.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. Reasoning post-training via SFT, GRPO or distillation improves math benchmarks but is not alignment-preserving by default. Regressions appear across safety, toxicity, stereotyping, ethics, privacy and OOD abstention. KL drift is offered as a diagnostic, not a causal explanation.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates SFT on reasoning traces, GRPO-style reasoning post-training, and distillation against matched instruction-tuned baselines. It does not apply the original few-example harmful or benign fine-tuning interventions, nor the token-depth methods.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported outcomes vary across reasoning pathway and prompt/task condition; the paper notes KL drift ranges from single digits to hundreds across prompt families. The evaluation includes multiple system-prompt regimes and model sizes.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Conditional sequence-level KL between reasoning models and their instruct baselines is large and prompt-family dependent: up to 474 for SFT vs 8.6–23 for base→instruct. Authors link larger drift to larger trust regressions.",
        "proposed_claim": "Reasoning post-training produces large sequence-level behavioural drift from the instruct baseline on trust-critical prompts. This does not show where in the response the drift occurs.",
