    "code_released": "https://github.com/prajak takini/ReasoningTrust",
    "peer_reviewed": "Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing",
    "problems": "The paper says its KL analysis is diagnostic rather than causal and is computed on a subset of models and prompt families. The safety result is reported as an aggregate across instruct and distill variants, with no uncertainty stated in the text. The study focuses on open-source models in the 1.5B–14B range and evaluates final outputs rather than hidden reasoning traces.",
    "rows_left_out": "Not applicable; 25 relevant results retained. Other trustworthiness measures, including toxicity, stereotyping, ethics, privacy, and OOD refusal, were left out because they do not measure harmful-request compliance or token-position concentration.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No fine-tuning on harmful examples."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Safety (HADES) not reported for the SFT (s1.1) or GRPO pathways, which are the clearest benign fine-tunes of instruct models. No dose or dataset-choice analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No per-token-position KL; KL is computed on sequences and on non-harmful trust prompt families."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No prefilling, suffix or decoding attacks."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No parameter localization."
      },
      {
